WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:02.210
INTRO:
Can you tell the difference between Coke and

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:03.270
Pepsi?

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:13.650
I suspect that most of you guys would say
yes, but statistically, this is not true.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:17.980
In fact, the average person’s ability to
differentiate between the two is not much

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.440
better than random chance.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.710
Researchers have been studying this phenomenon
for decades at this point with one of the

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.110
first studies coming from the University of
Wichita.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:31.980
In the first part of this study, participants
were given Coca-Cola, Pepsi, or RC Cola and

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.400
they were asked to guess which one was which.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:40.160
Only 41% of participants were able to correctly
identify Coca-Cola while 38% identified Pepsi

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.810
and 35% identified RC Cola.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:46.860
Off the bat, these aren’t very good results
given that the majority failed to identify

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.390
any of them correctly.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:52.660
But, this gets way worse when we take a look
at the second part of the study.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:56.950
The researchers had a feeling that the reason
more people were able to guess Coca-Cola correctly

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:00.420
was not because they were able to tell the
difference but simply because they were more

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.090
likely to guess Coca-Cola.

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:06.560
To confirm this, the researchers gave the
participants three cups of the same soda without

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.960
telling them that it was the same soda.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.229
And then, the participants were asked to identify
which one was which.

00:01:12.229 --> 00:01:15.939
As you would guess, the results were quite
similar to that of the first part.

00:01:15.939 --> 00:01:21.280
45% of participants were able to correctly
identify Coca-Cola while 32% identified Pepsi

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.170
and 25% identified RC Cola.

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:27.979
But while most people can’t statistically
tell the difference between Coca-Cola and

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:32.159
Pepsi, 71% say that they would choose Coke
over Pepsi.

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:35.590
One of the top reasons they cite for this
preference is a seemingly larger caffeine

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:40.029
boost which is quite ironic given that Pepsi
is the one with more caffeine.

00:01:40.029 --> 00:01:44.950
Clearly, Coke has won the branding wars by
a mile, but the funny part is that it’s

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.240
not just competitors who can’t overcome
Coke’s branding.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.119
Even Coca-Cola themselves can’t overcome
Coke’s branding.

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:55.209
So, here’s the most notable attempts of
companies trying to beat Coke and why they

00:01:55.209 --> 00:01:56.209
all failed.

00:01:56.209 --> 00:02:03.600
THE PEPSI CHALLENGE:
Likely one of the most valiant efforts against

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.810
Coke’s dominance was the Pepsi challenge
which made it’s debut in the mid 1970s.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:12.980
By this point, Pepsi and Coke had already
been fighting for market share for over 70

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:13.980
years.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.470
Yet, it seemed like Coke always had the upperhand.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:21.780
No matter what ads Pepsi ran, who they marketed
to, or what endorsements they had, the majority

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.230
of people simply chose Coke.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:29.090
This made Pepsi quite mad, not just because
all of their efforts were wasted but because

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:31.910
they strongly believed that they had the superior
product.

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:35.840
They hadn’t just run a few dozen or even
a few hundred taste tests.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.300
Pepsi had run 3000 blind taste tests, and
the majority preferred Pepsi.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:45.120
So, in 1975, Pepsi would publicly call out
Coke for having inferior taste and challenge

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.650
viewers to participate in the Pepsi challenge.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:53.010
They set up pop up taste tests at malls, department
stores, parks, basically anywhere they could,

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.790
and they had random people blindly taste Pepsi
and Coke.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:00.710
As you would guess, most of them would choose
Pepsi, and Pepsi would film their live reactions

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:02.350
and compile them into commercials.

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:07.340
These commercials were actually super effective
as many people decided to give Pepsi a shot.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:11.710
In fact, by 1983, Pepsi was outselling Coke
in supermarkets.

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:15.710
There’s rumors that after seeing the success
of the Pepsi challenge, Coca-Cola started

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:17.960
filming their own Coke challange.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:21.540
But they never aired this footage because
the majority of participants actually ended

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:22.650
up choosing Pepsi.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:27.790
So, it doesn’t look like Pepsi was faking
their results, but despite this, Pepsi was

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:31.350
never able to overtake Coca-Cola in overall
marketshare.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:36.160
The closest they got was in 1980 when Pepsi
marketshare hovered in the high 20s and Coca-Cola

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.380
market share hovered in the mid 30s.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:42.069
One of the key reasons for this was Coca-Cola’s
far larger reach thanks to their dominance

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:44.400
amongst soda machines and fast food restaurants.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.569
But, while Coca-Cola was able to keep their
lead, the Pepsi challenge definitely hurt

00:03:48.569 --> 00:03:54.490
their ego, and it wouldn’t be long until
they fired back with their own new product.

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:59.840
NEW COKE:
In the early 1980s, Coca-Cola began running

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.950
their own internal taste tests, but instead
of having people choose between Pepsi and

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:08.590
Coke, they had people choose between Pepsi,
Coke, and a new formulation of Coke.

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:12.000
Clearly, Coca-Cola was convinced that their
original product was starting to lose its

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.270
flair and that it was time to revamp their
formula.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:20.280
This was obviously a bold move, and Coca-Cola
definitely did not take this task lightly.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:25.580
They spent years tweaking the flavor and they
conducted 190,000 blind taste tests.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:28.900
And it wasn’t until they could definitively
prove that their new formulation was the preferred

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.020
taste that they decided to make the change.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:36.810
And with that, on April 23, 1985, Coca-Cola’s
CEO, Roberto Goizueta, proudly announced the

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:38.849
launch of New Coke.

00:04:38.849 --> 00:04:42.639
Despite their grand expecations though, new
coke started hitting obstacles from the second

00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:44.039
it was launched.

00:04:44.039 --> 00:04:47.999
First of all, the launch of new coke itself
was an admission that Pepsi did in fact have

00:04:47.999 --> 00:04:49.319
the superior product.

00:04:49.319 --> 00:04:53.620
And secondly, new coke was noticeabley sweeter
than the original coke just like Pepsi.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:57.139
And this was yet another admission that Pepsi
was right all along.

00:04:57.139 --> 00:04:59.340
Pepsi’s executives were ecstatic.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:02.830
After 87 years, they had finally gotten a
response out of Coca-Cola.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:06.401
Pepsi’s executives were actually so hyped
that they gave their employees the rest of

00:05:06.401 --> 00:05:07.569
the day off.

00:05:07.569 --> 00:05:10.620
Meanwhile, Coca-Cola’s executives were also
super optimistic.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:15.430
The CEO called new Coke “the surest move
ever made” and the president declared that

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:18.770
new Coke was the most confident decision he
had ever made.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:22.270
While Coke was publicly admitting that Pepsi
was right, they now had a product that was

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:23.460
definitively better.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:27.279
There’s no way 190,000 taste tests were
wrong right?

00:05:27.279 --> 00:05:32.960
Well, the taste tests were indeed most likely
accurate, but consumers didn’t care.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.150
Coke fans didn’t want a better Coke, they
just wanted the Coke.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:41.360
And given that Coca-Cola had discontinued
the old version, consumers had no choice but

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:42.860
to voice their opinions.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:48.991
CLASSIC COKE:
In the weeks following the announcement, 5000

00:05:48.991 --> 00:05:53.550
customers called the company on a daily basis
requesting that original coke was brought

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:54.550
back.

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:58.560
Within a few months, this number actually
grew to 8000 calls per day which forced Coca-Cola

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.690
to hire more phone operators.

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:04.419
Aside from all these angry phone calls, people
were publicly showcasing their hatred for

00:06:04.419 --> 00:06:05.659
new Coke.

00:06:05.659 --> 00:06:09.939
For example, consumers across America were
pouring new Coke down sewer drains.

00:06:09.939 --> 00:06:13.900
And one guy in Seatle even filed a lawsuit
against Coca-Cola to bring back the original

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:14.900
Coke.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:19.599
Of course, this lawsuit didn’t go anywhere,
but it was a clear sign of just how much people

00:06:19.599 --> 00:06:20.900
despised the move.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:25.780
At first, Coca-Cola tried to ignore the resistance
as much as possible likely thinking that it

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:27.650
would go away sooner or later.

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:32.729
But, as the resistance just continued to get
stronger, Coca-Cola finally gave in just 79

00:06:32.729 --> 00:06:35.009
days after announcing New Coke.

00:06:35.009 --> 00:06:39.120
The president of Coca-Cola declared that while
they had believed that New Coke was the way

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.639
forward, in the end, their boss is the consumer,
and it was clear what the consumer wanted.

00:06:43.639 --> 00:06:46.710
Coca-Cola would go on to bring back the original
recipe as Coca-Cola classic.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:51.039
And if you’ve ever wondered why old bottles
of coke say the word “classic” on them,

00:06:51.039 --> 00:06:52.199
this is why.

00:06:52.199 --> 00:06:56.380
Almost instantaneously, the original formula
would overtake the new formula in sales and

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:59.449
regain its positions as the world’s most
popular soda.

00:06:59.449 --> 00:07:01.430
Coca-Cola didn’t just kill off New Coke
though.

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:06.110
They rebranded New Coke as Coke II in 1990
and they kept it around till 2002.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:10.240
While this was likely not a very fun experience
for Coca-Cola, this actually turned out to

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:11.879
be a massive benefit.

00:07:11.879 --> 00:07:16.229
The time away from original coke showed people
just how much they liked the soda, and loyalty

00:07:16.229 --> 00:07:18.839
to the drink become stronger than ever before.

00:07:18.839 --> 00:07:22.879
This positive outcome has prompted many to
speculate whether the entire fiasco was planned

00:07:22.879 --> 00:07:23.879
from the beginning.

00:07:23.879 --> 00:07:26.909
But I think Coca-Cola’s former president
says it best.

00:07:26.909 --> 00:07:31.289
“The truth is we’re not that dumb and
we’re not that smart.”

00:07:31.289 --> 00:07:37.699
WHY NEW COKE FAILED?:
In retrospect, it’s clear that neither Pepsi

00:07:37.699 --> 00:07:43.509
nor Coca-Cola’s attempts at displacing the
original soda were successful, but why?

00:07:43.509 --> 00:07:47.479
Every statistic that we know tells us that
New Coke and Pepsi tasted better and that

00:07:47.479 --> 00:07:50.360
people can’t even identify the drink based
on the taste.

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:54.129
So, why in the world was there such a negative
reaction to new Coke?

00:07:54.129 --> 00:07:58.990
Well, the answer boils down to two parts:
the logical side and the emotional side.

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:02.529
From a logical perspective, you could argue
that a taste test is not a very good measure

00:08:02.529 --> 00:08:05.319
of what people would like to drink on a regular
basis.

00:08:05.319 --> 00:08:09.110
One of the main reasons that both Pepsi and
New Coke performed better in taste tests was

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:11.090
because they were both sweeter than classic
Coke.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:14.740
And when you’re only tasting a few sips
of each soda, most people are naturally going

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:16.149
to choose the soda that’s sweeter.

00:08:16.149 --> 00:08:20.611
But, when we’re talking about a full can
of soda or even multiple cans, it’s likely

00:08:20.611 --> 00:08:24.149
that people reach flavor fatigue much faster
with the sweeter soda.

00:08:24.149 --> 00:08:28.069
As a result, while people may prefer sweeter
options in a taste test, they tend to prefer

00:08:28.069 --> 00:08:29.939
classic Coke over the long term.

00:08:29.939 --> 00:08:33.490
The only problem with this line of reasoning
though is that it assumes that people can

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:37.710
discern between Coke and Pepsi which is stastically
not true.

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:41.479
Now of course, if you’re drinking Pepsi
and Coke side by side like the taste testsers,

00:08:41.479 --> 00:08:44.300
you can likely tell that they don’t taste
exactly alike.

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:48.510
But, if you were randomly given one or the
other, you likely wouldn’t be able to correctly

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:50.570
guess what you were given.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:54.060
Considering this, people’s preference for
classic coke likely has less to do with logical

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:57.290
reasoning and more to do with emotional reasoning.

00:08:57.290 --> 00:09:00.700
And a great example of this is the results
of yet another study.

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:04.420
In this study, the researchers gave participants
two bottles of soda.

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:08.210
One was labeled with the letter L while the
other was labeled with the letter S, but the

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:10.850
contents of the two bottles were identical.

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:13.630
Given that it’s the same soda, there should
theoretically be no statistically significant

00:09:13.630 --> 00:09:15.930
difference between people’s preference for
either soda.

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:19.430
Yet, participants overwhelmingly preferred
the soda with the letter S.

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:23.250
The explanation for this is actually that
the average person tends to like the letter

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:27.520
S more than the letter L meaning that it has
nothing to do with the soda itself.

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:31.530
Some researchers further proved this by giving
people Pepsi in a Coca-Cola bottle and Coca-Cola

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:34.750
in a Pepsi bottle, and the participants didn’t
notice.

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:40.990
So clearly, taste was not the factor that
was holding back any of these drinks.

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:44.690
NOSTALGIA:
In the end, I think the main reason that Pepsi

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:49.490
nor Coca-Cola nor any other soda brand can
ever displace classic Coke is due to nostalgia.

00:09:49.490 --> 00:09:53.360
In our extremely fast paced world, everything
around is rapidly changing.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:58.260
We don’t have the same phones or computers
that we had 10 years ago, but we all still

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:01.640
drink the same soda that our parents and their
parents drank.

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:06.020
And as we all grow older, soda is one of the
few things that we come across on a daily

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:09.630
basis that remind us of our childhood and
how things used to be.

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:14.100
For example, I wasn’t around when New Coke
came out, but I do specifically remember an

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:15.860
instance relating to classic Coke.

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:21.220
Way back in 2007, I was at Seaworld and we
had both Coke and Sprite cans, and I remember

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:23.970
wondering why the Coke can said classic but
the sprite can didn’t.

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:29.630
I was only 5 years old at the time and I remember
being sad that my drink of choice, Sprite,

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:30.630
was not a classic.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:34.970
It wasn’t till 10 years later that I actually
found out what the classic meant.

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:38.900
And now, whenever I hear about new coke or
coke classic, I always think about going to

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:40.330
Seaworld as a kid.

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:46.130
I suspect this nostalgic feeling is way stronger
for someone who’s 50, 70, 90 years old.

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:49.420
Coke is one of the few things that remind
them of their own childhoods way back in the

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:50.960
1930s or 40s.

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:54.650
And as long as Coke carries this nostalgic
significance, it’s gonna be nearly impossible

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:55.650
to displace.

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:57.850
Do you like Coke or Pepsi?

00:10:57.850 --> 00:10:59.150
Comment that down below.

